Perf baselines: compare new vs old runs under controlled slowdown

Status: not implemented. `pnpm perf` only asserts absolute `BUDGET` ceilings in
e2e/perf/editor.perf.ts. Results are logged/attached per run; nothing stores a
reference or diffs against a prior run. CI does not run `pnpm perf` today.

Why this exists (motive)

Absolute budgets absorb machine noise with generous headroom (~2x observed at
recalibration), so they only catch large cliffs. They cannot answer "did this
PR make typing 20% worse on the same harness?" Numbers already fluctuate under
identical PERF_CPU throttle (dev build, MSW, runner load), so a single run's
absolute ms is a weak signal for creeping regressions. Without a same-condition
baseline, "unreliable numbers" stays a permanent excuse and small editor
regressions land until they blow past the ceiling.

Proposal

TLDR: use consistent machine(s) for testing, run the test multiple times for higer accuracy (and compare to baseline). and since its time costly, only run after major checkpoints have been reached (multiple feature rich commits/development regarding the editor workflow) or before releases 

Keep the existing absolute budgets as a catastrophic backstop. Add a relative
comparison layer on top:

1. Persist a structured JSON snapshot per budget case (openMs, INP /
   blockingPerKeystroke, saveCycle blocking, scroll FPS, document weight,
   PERF_CPU, Chromium version, commit SHA). Reuse what reportMetrics already
   emits.
2. Store a reference baseline from a controlled environment — prefer one fixed
   CI runner class (or a labeled self-hosted runner), not "whoever ran it
   last on a laptop." Update the baseline on main after a green perf job, or
   check in a periodically refreshed artifact.
3. On PR / local compare: fail (or warn) if a metric worsens beyond a relative
   threshold (e.g. blockingPerKeystroke > baseline * 1.25) under the same
   PERF_CPU and same fixtures. Report deltas in the job summary / Playwright
   attachment so humans see "new vs old," not only pass/fail.
4. Do not assert on the opt-in V8 profile cases (typingProfile /
   saveCycleProfile); they remain forensic dumps. Relative gates apply only to
   the four budget specs in editor.perf.ts.
5. Optional later: multi-run median (N=3) before compare, to dampen single-run
   spikes without widening absolute budgets forever.

Pros

- Catches creeping regressions that absolute ceilings miss until they are huge.
- Same controlled slowdown becomes meaningful: delta is the signal, absolute ms
  is context.
- Forces a shared harness story (fixture, CPU rate, build mode) instead of
  tribal recalibration comments alone.
- Absolute budgets stay as a safety net for "completely broken."

Cons / pitfalls

- Cross-machine baselines are worse than none: laptop vs GHA will false-fail.
  Relative compare only works if reference and candidate share environment class.
- CI cost and flake: near-limit cases already take minutes; adding N repeats or
  a dedicated perf job lengthens the pipeline. Shared ubuntu-latest is also
  noisier than a pinned runner.
- Dev-build inflation remains; relative gates still measure Vite/React-dev, not
  production UX. Do not treat deltas as user-facing SLOs.
- MSW save-cycle work on the main thread can move independently of real collab
  servers — relative fails there need the same caveat as today's absolute
  saveCycle budget.
- Stale or hand-edited baselines become a new footgun; need a clear "how
  baseline is refreshed" rule (main-only artifact vs checked-in file).
- Threshold tuning is political: too tight = flake, too loose = back to
  absolute-only.

Related current behavior

- e2e/perf/editor.perf.ts — BUDGET + reportMetrics
- e2e/perf/metrics.ts — instrumentation; no history
- e2e/perf/typingProfile.perf.ts / saveCycleProfile.perf.ts — PERF_PROFILE=1
  diagnostics, assert nothing
- .github/workflows/ci.yml — does not run pnpm perf
